/-!
# Level one: weight 12N forms are P(j) Δ^N in ℂ((q))

Theorem statement: `ModularForm.exists_qExpansion_eq_aeval_mul_pow_levelOne`
(`dc26842e-3c37-5ba0-aaba-e83bcd87e7b3`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `74ab1c20-fb2b-5f2f-88be-47b82744a75a`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a natural number and let $F$ be a modular form of weight $12N$ for the full modular group
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ (an element of `ModularForm 𝒮ℒ (12 * (N : ℤ))`, the weight being the
integer $12N$). The assertion is that there exists a polynomial $P \in \mathbb{C}[X]$ whose
`natDegree` is at most $N$ such that, after the canonical embedding of power series into Laurent
series $\mathbb{C}((q))$, the $q$-expansion of $F$ at width $1$ satisfies $$\widetilde{F} =
P\bigl(\widetilde{E_4}^{\,3}/\widetilde{\Delta}\bigr)\cdot \widetilde{\Delta}^{\,N},$$ where
$\widetilde{\,\cdot\,}$ denotes `qExpansion 1` of the underlying function on the upper half-plane,
$E_4$ is the normalised weight-$4$ Eisenstein series `ModularForm.E₄`, $\Delta$ is
`ModularForm.discriminant`, the quotient and the $N$-th power are formed in the field of Laurent
series, and $P$ is evaluated at that quotient through the $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure of
$\mathbb{C}((q))$. Thus the bound on $P$ is on its natural degree, and the identity is an identity
of Laurent series, not of modular forms.

This is the level-one structure statement in $q$-expansion form: every weight-$12N$ form for
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ is a polynomial of degree at most $N$ in $j = E_4^3/\Delta$ multiplied
by $\Delta^N$. It is used in the integrality arguments for $q$-expansion coefficients on modular
curves, for instance in the results establishing integrality of coefficient data over an algebra
generated by $j$-series data and in the Hasse-polynomial descent step.

